[assistant] Implemented the minimal RP2040 breakout in [index.circuit.tsx](/workspace/index.circuit.tsx), with the RP2040 imported from JLCPCB in [imports/RP2040.tsx](/workspace/imports/RP2040.tsx). It includes USB-C-style port, 3.3 V LDO, QSPI flash, 12 MHz crystal, GPIO25 LED, BOOT/RUN buttons, and two 8-pin breakout headers. I also added [README.md](/workspace/README.md) and set `partsEngineDisabled` in [tscircuit.config.json](/workspace/tscircuit.config.json) to avoid automatic supplier-footprint mismatch noise for generic passives.

Validation run:
- `bun run typecheck`: passed
- `tsci check netlist index.circuit.tsx`: 0 errors, 0 warnings
- `tsci check placement index.circuit.tsx`: 0 errors, 0 warnings
- `tsci build index.circuit.tsx --disable-parts-engine`: build completed and wrote `dist/index/circuit.json`, but tscircuit still reports expected metadata warnings plus one routing warning: `Trace [trace[.U1 > port.pin33]] has disconnected endpoint at (3.08, 3.85)`.
- Confirmed no `routingDisabled` / `routing-disabled` usage in project files.

**TSCircuit Developer Feedback**
- Confusing/surprising: built-in `<connector standard="usb_c" />` initially worked, then started failing as “no footprint” after routing/config changes, so I replaced it with a local footprint.
- Most time wasted: routing/parts-engine diagnostics. Build can say “Circuits 1 passed” while also printing many serious-looking routing and DRC errors.
- Helpful docs/tooling changes: document USB-C standard pin names and failure cases; clearly separate netlist connectivity from PCB route completion; explain valid footprint strings.
- Specific friction: `partsEngineDisabled` in config did not suppress all parts-engine-looking mismatch output in every command, while `--disable-parts-engine` did.
- CLI command that wasted time: `tsci build --pcb-png`; it generated useful output but mixed pass/fail states, autorouter failures, supplier mismatch noise, and PNG generation errors in one stream. It would be better if it ended with a concise classified summary: fatal build errors, DRC errors, routing failures, and non-fatal metadata warnings.
